      "parsed_output": {
        "files_changed": [
          "src/main/java/org/apache/commons/io/FilenameUtils.java",
          "src/test/java/org/apache/commons/io/FilenameUtilsTestCase.java"
        ],
        "patch_notes": "src/main/java/org/apache/commons/io/FilenameUtils.java: (1) added imports java.util.Arrays, java.util.List, java.util.regex.Matcher, java.util.regex.Pattern; (2) added constants EMPTY_STRING_ARRAY, IPV4_PATTERN, IPV4_MAX_OCTET_VALUE, IPV6_MAX_HEX_GROUPS, IPV6_MAX_HEX_DIGITS_PER_GROUP, MAX_UNSIGNED_SHORT, BASE_16, REG_NAME_PART_PATTERN; (3) in getPrefixLength(), the UNC/double-separator branch now computes hostnamePart = filename.substring(2, pos-1) and returns NOT_FOUND unless isValidHostName(hostnamePart) is true; (4) added private helper methods isValidHostName, isIPv4Address, isIPv6Address, isRFC3986HostName (ported from the upstream Apache Commons IO 2.7 fix for this class of bug). src/test/java/org/apache/commons/io/FilenameUtilsTestCase.java: added testNormalize_CVE_2021_29425_pathTraversal(), asserting normalize()/getPrefixLength() reject '//../foo', '\\\\..\\foo', '//..\\foo', and malformed-hostname UNC prefixes like '//-server/foo' and '//.server/foo'.",
        "regression_commands": [
          "mvn -q -B -Dtest=FilenameUtilsTestCase test",
          "mvn -q -B -Dtest=FileSystemUtilsTestCase test",
          "mvn -B -DskipTests package"
        ],
        "risk_notes": "Behavior change is narrowly scoped to inputs with a leading double path separator (UNC-style paths) whose apparent host/share segment is not a syntactically valid RFC 3986 host name or IPv6 literal — such inputs now yield null from normalize()/normalizeNoEndSeparator() and -1 from getPrefixLength() instead of a previously-unvalidated result. Legitimate UNC paths ('//server/share/...', numeric-IP hosts, etc.) are unaffected, confirmed by the full pre-existing FilenameUtilsTestCase suite (38/38 pass, including the '//server/...' cases already in the suite) and FileSystemUtilsTestCase (28/28 pass). A full `mvn test` run shows 4 pre-existing failures (FileFilterTestCase.testCanRead/testCanWrite, FileUtilsCleanDirectoryTestCase.testThrowsOnCannotDeleteFile/testThrowsOnNullList) that reproduce identically on the unpatched baseline (verified via git stash) — they stem from file-permission-enforcement tests running as root in the container, unrelated to this change.",
        "status": "patched",
        "summary": "Fixed CWE-022 path traversal in FilenameUtils.normalize()/getPrefixLength() (the same root cause as CVE-2021-29425). For UNC-style inputs beginning with a double separator (e.g. '//../foo', '\\\\..\\foo'), getPrefixLength previously computed the prefix length purely from the position of the next separator, without validating that the intervening text was a plausible host/share name. That let a '..' segment be silently swallowed into the 'prefix' portion of the path, where it is never touched by doNormalize's '..'-collapsing logic — so normalize() returned the traversal sequence unchanged/mishandled instead of rejecting it, letting callers that trust normalize() to keep paths inside an allowed directory be bypassed. FileSystemUtils.performCommand/openUnixStat build OS commands from normalize()'d, attacker-influenced paths, so those call sites are covered too, but the fix is entirely in FilenameUtils at the trust boundary.\n\nFix (matches the upstream Apache Commons IO 2.7 fix for IO-556/IO-559): getPrefixLength() now extracts the candidate host/share substring between the leading '//' or '\\\\' and the following separator and validates it via isValidHostName() (RFC 3986 host name or IPv6 literal) before treating it as a legitimate prefix. If the substring isn't a valid host name (e.g. '..', '-server', '.server'), getPrefixLength returns NOT_FOUND (-1), which makes doNormalize() return null instead of an unsanitized/ambiguous path. This blocks the highlighted '//../foo' path plus same-class variants: backslash form ('\\\\..\\foo'), mixed separators ('//..\\foo'), and malformed host names in general (leading hyphen, leading dot, etc.), while preserving legitimate UNC paths like '//server/a/b' (existing test suite covers these and continues to pass)."
      }
